.TH STRIDER 1
.SH NAME
strider \- a walking-machine combat sim
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/strider
[
.B -t
|
.B -b
|
.B -s
] [
.B -q
] [
.B -S
|
.B -D
] [
.B -n
.I frames
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Strider
is a combat sim in the shape that genre took:
a throttle rather than a speed, so the machine takes time to get moving
and time to stop;
legs that turn slower than the torso they carry;
a torso that aims independently of them;
three weapon groups that trade damage against heat and ammunition;
a heat scale that will shut the machine down if it is ignored;
and armour that is lost a section at a time rather than a point at a
time.
.PP
Where
.IR gorge (1)
is a corridor that slides past a fixed eye, this is a heightfield with a
machine walking over it, and the eye is the cockpit.
.SS Controls
.TP
.B "left, right"
Turn the legs.
Slowly \- this is what the machine is standing on.
.TP
.B "up, down"
Throttle.
.B =
and
.B -
step it, and
.B 0
sets it to zero.
.TP
.BR z ", " Tab
Twist the torso left and right respectively, up to seventy degrees
either side of the legs.
.B c
centres it.
This is the mechanic the whole genre is built on: you can walk one way
and shoot another.
.TP
.BR x ", " . ", space"
Fire the selected group.
Held, it fires again as each group cycles.
.TP
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", " 3
Select laser, autocannon or missiles.
The laser needs no ammunition and makes the most heat; missiles fire a
salvo of six.
.TP
.B e
Cycle the target.
Not Tab: that twists the torso, and a binding taken twice is a binding
taken once.
.PP
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SS The other machines
They walk rather than slide.
One sine drives the whole gait, advanced by ground covered and not by
the clock, so a machine standing still does not march on the spot and
one backing away steps backwards: the legs swing against each other, the
leading foot lifts, the hips drop at mid stride when both feet are down,
and the arms swing against the legs.
They start out of step with one another, because a lance walking in
lockstep looks like one machine drawn four times.
.PP
Their torsos lead their legs.
Legs turn at a fixed rate and the torso covers whatever the legs have
not turned yet, so a machine that has just been flanked is aiming at you
long before it has walked round \- which is the same thing your own
torso twist does, seen from the outside.
.PP
A destroyed machine goes down over about a second rather than vanishing:
the legs fold, the torso pitches forward over them, the head is thrown
out ahead.
.SS The display
Heat and throttle read along the bottom, the weapon groups top left with
their remaining rounds, the target's range and centre-torso armour along
the top, and the machine's own armour as a diagram top right \- six
boxes in the shape of the machine, each falling and changing colour as
that section is spent.
The radar bottom right is turned so that up is where the
.I torso
points, which is what a pilot needs and is not the same as north.
.PP
All of it is drawn over the 3-D with the depth test off.
A head-up display that the terrain could occlude would be no use at all.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/strider.dis
.EE
.PP
It renders through the software provider by default, like
.IR gorge (1)
and for the same reason: the GPU path composes the frame wrongly, and a
cockpit sim whose head-up display gets painted over is the worst case
for that.
See BUGS in
.IR gorge (1),
which this shares.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/strider.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gorge (1),
.IR draw3d (2),
