.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B -b
draws as fast as it will go and reports what a frame costs.
On this host, the full grid at 640 by 480 through
.B /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
with no window manager is about 2225 microseconds a frame \- but see
BUGS in
.IR gorge (1)
before believing that number means anything about the picture.
.B -n
sets the frames timed,
.B -S
forces the software provider (the default) and
.B -D
the device one, which is faster and wrong.
.PP
.B -t
runs the self test, which needs no window; it draws a fifth of the grid
and one frame in ten, for the reasons
.IR gorge (1)
gives.
Thirty-eight checks: that the ground never steps; that the throttle is a
throttle and not a speed, and that the machine stops where it is set;
that the torso twists without the legs following and stops at its limit,
that the legs turn without the torso following, and that the aim is the
sum;
that firing makes heat, that heat sheds, that full heat shuts the
machine down, that a shut down machine ignores its throttle, and that it
comes back at half heat;
that a group spends a round and honours its own cooldown, that a laser
spends none, that an empty group fires nothing and that a missile group
fires a salvo;
that a laser takes armour off what it is pointed at and misses what it
is not, and leaves a beam to draw;
that a machine with no arms or legs is still standing and a destroyed
centre torso is not;
that targeting cycles only what is still up;
that the other machines close the range and shoot back;
that a frame draws something;
that an enemy in front of the cockpit puts pixels on the screen while
one behind it does not;
that a machine standing still does not advance its stride and a walking
one does, and that the stride stays in range;
that three different points in the stride produce three different
frames, which is the check that the gait reaches the screen rather than
only the state;
and that a destroyed machine falls over several frames, is still drawn
while it falls, and ends flat.
.SH BUGS
The last two checks exist because of a bug that thirty-eight state
checks could not see.
An
.B if(0)
left in front of the loop that draws the other machines \- from an
experiment isolating which geometry provoked
.B "bad ratzerr1"
\- hid every enemy for a whole commit, and everything still passed:
they walked, they closed, they fired, the radar showed them and the
reticle boxed empty sky.
A test that only ever asks about state cannot see a thing that is not
drawn.
Putting the
.B if(0)
back fails the check, which is how it is known to test anything.
.PP
The same experiment left the frame counter comparing against a sky
colour written out by hand, so when the sky was later brightened
.B "a frame draws something"
became true of a frame that drew nothing.
It reads the sky out of a corner of the frame now.
.PP
The terrain cull is
.IR draw3d (2)'s
own
.I project
and not trigonometry of this file's, and that is not tidiness.
A cull written from a separate idea of where the camera points can
disagree with the camera, and the first one here did \- it kept exactly
the cells the camera was not looking at, and the world came out empty
with the head-up display drawn over it perfectly.
.PP
The cull is also load bearing for a reason that is not visible.
A cell far off the view axis projects to enormous screen coordinates,
and that drives
.IR polyfill (2)'s
rational-z accumulator out of range: thousands of
.B "bad ratzerr1"
lines a frame, a diagnostic that has been in
.B fillpoly
since Plan 9.
.IR Gorge (1)
never hit it because its corridor stays near the axis.
With the cull the count is zero; the underlying fault is not fixed and
is not this program's.
.PP
Damage lands on a section chosen arithmetically rather than on the
section the shot actually struck.
What matters to the game is that armour is spent unevenly and that the
centre torso is what kills, and both of those are true, but a shot to
the leg can take the arm.
.PP
The other machines all behave the same way and have the same loadout.
There is no lance, no formation and no retreat.
.PP
Missiles fly straight.
The salvo spreads, but nothing homes.
